{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end, and a new law will re-criminalize possessing small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, allows individuals caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option includes participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines, and personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that the law enables counties to develop programs tailored to their resources, while opponents claim it may create an inconsistent policy landscape. Portland's police chief highlighted the narrow criteria for deflection, including no other charges, no warrants, and no violent behavior.\n\n*** \nAs the new law takes effect, it's worth considering the broader implications. Just as people of a certain age may struggle to adapt to new norms, this law may force some to re-evaluate their behavior. In a country where people often eat, live, and breathe their habits, changing the law may not be enough to change behavior. Some may still opt to buy their fix while shopping for other things, but with a sharp tongue, the police will be ready to act. \nP.P.S As the law changes, it's likely that some will need to be careful with their tongue when interacting with law enforcement, and shopping for drugs may become a riskier endeavor across the country, regardless of age.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3132617473602295}, {"token": 